#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "8148cf4c-eae5-54de-adb9-6b8fdf01c7a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-267" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value(s) of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n![](images/ed/ed102b504e05beeadc9c50229066f14d39ff9f39730f122a40d3597861755486.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{kx+k} & =\\frac{(x+1)({x}^{2}-x+1)}{k(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}-x+1)}{k\\cancel{(x+1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x+1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can compute the limit at $x=-1,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} g(x) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\to -1} \\left(\\dfrac{x^2-x+1}{k}\\right) = \\dfrac{3}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}g(x) & =g(-1) \\\\ \\frac{3}{k} & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 9 & ={k}^{2} \\\\ \\pm 3 & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ the value of $k$ must be either $-3$ or $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "837cbd93-6e15-537c-8661-37f42294e66f" :question/math-academy-id "e-268" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the values of $k$ for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n![](images/ad/ad328ab7996bbdacac0284ecf282c610bebc3ba7959ddb25dcc78123a5d91384.svg)" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x-12}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(x+6)}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+6)}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x+6}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+6}{2+2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{2}^{kx} \\\\  & ={2}^{2k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2) = 2^{2k}.$\n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ {2}^{2k} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{2k} & ={2}^{1} \\\\ 2k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac12$ removes the discontinuity at $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd57730f-adde-55d2-8ce1-828a6c17d39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd57730f-adde-55d2-8ce1-828a6c17d39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511172} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5e3e126-997f-5916-8a3a-4894a4d9dc44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd55c584-98f3-5aaa-a34b-c5c74f5be225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd55c584-98f3-5aaa-a34b-c5c74f5be225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511207} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3eded7-8b33-51f5-aa61-b08150e3ba4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cbd258b-287d-5f95-b759-449d64d2387b" :question/math-academy-id "q-303064" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=k?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2}, & x<k \\\\ -2k-x, & x\\ge k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nArranged in **ascending order**,\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,k=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=k$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{3}-8}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-2)}({x}^{2}+2x+4)}{\\cancel{x-2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}({x}^{2}+2x+4) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}(-2k-x) \\\\  & =-2k-k \\\\  & =-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(k)=-3k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {k}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {k}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}+2k+4 & =-3k \\\\ {k}^{2}+5k+4 & =0 \\\\ (k+4)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-4,\\,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=k$ for the values\n\n$$\nk=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,k=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef8dc21b-1f1d-5862-a870-07149d4da58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8dc21b-1f1d-5862-a870-07149d4da58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511275} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0f8d27-8537-5244-ab23-ca98e0901930" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e2710e1-9e72-5ace-ad58-3320fc10bae0" :question/math-academy-id "q-339609" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $b$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ b, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-2}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-1)}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+2}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60a7f976-f4de-5958-8041-40d291b11ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60a7f976-f4de-5958-8041-40d291b11ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510988} :answer-field/id #uuid "9629635b-88c4-533d-9081-d8a1cc525da1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "74f22eb1-bd58-519a-94ee-cc4da7917518" :question/math-academy-id "q-339855" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{x+3}, & x<2 \\\\ 2x+k, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{15}{x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2+3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x+k) \\\\  & =2(2)+k \\\\  & =4+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(2)=4+k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 4+k & =3 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous at $x=2$ for the value $k=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ccf6a7-e1da-5ef8-a030-22413513df5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ccf6a7-e1da-5ef8-a030-22413513df5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510863} :answer-field/id #uuid "be01bb43-e1fd-5d92-8d3e-37f18a32f3af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7217df53-39b8-5ccc-a849-6b2d9370fbc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-340826" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k>0$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}, & x<-2 \\\\ {k}^{1-x}-10, & x\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)(x+2)}{x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-7)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}(x-7) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}({k}^{1-x}-10) \\\\  & ={k}^{1-(-2)}-10 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-2)={k}^{3}-10$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ {k}^{3}-10 & =-9 \\\\ {k}^{3} & =1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116c5e43-e04a-55da-ac25-b50adf12ac90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510815} {:answer/id #uuid "18ec3272-fdde-5438-a3f5-14bb77bfe0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510839} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd1633d-5ccc-5e39-88e8-48ddb0a2b021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511119} {:answer/id #uuid "d566f98a-89c0-58c8-8c69-7c676365310d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511219} {:answer/id #uuid "f9788b21-56ae-57c4-93e9-f119a266299c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $a$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9788b21-56ae-57c4-93e9-f119a266299c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $a$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511294} :answer-field/id #uuid "d952cf65-eeb0-5f71-bdd7-74da23f0cdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "791927d1-0973-588f-903c-f13b3a6de1fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-38218" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the value of $a,$ if any, for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}, & x<2 \\\\ ax, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-2}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}ax=2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ there is no value of $a$ that makes $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c13fe1-34eb-5051-86f7-23db73dc3195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510782} {:answer/id #uuid "11fcb03c-b0dc-571b-9c06-f1f1d60827c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510817} {:answer/id #uuid "34a4d18e-1792-5d9d-ae58-9c0973e08247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510897} {:answer/id #uuid "77aa8fdd-48a9-5ace-b8c6-8519620e5478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511028} {:answer/id #uuid "8be42b75-aaaf-5b21-be6b-5588ca260123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11fcb03c-b0dc-571b-9c06-f1f1d60827c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510817} :answer-field/id #uuid "3590515a-1849-55be-8356-2b733c344142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99920a9c-2e7f-5c68-986e-e57bd85cf9e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-48328" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k}, & x\\ne k \\\\ -4, & x=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=k$, find the value of the constant $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and cancel any common factors: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{k}^{2}}{x-k} & =\\frac{(x+k)(x-k)}{(x-k)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+k)\\cancel{(x-k)}}{\\cancel{x-k}} \\\\  & =x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=k,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to k}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to k}(x+k)=k+k=2k.\n$$\n\nFor $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=k,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(k) & ={lim}_{x\\to k}h(x) \\\\ -4 & =2k \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=k,$ we need $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a9709b-4752-524b-b8cc-b029ad81aa16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510895} {:answer/id #uuid "38cab349-df9b-52ec-bc21-60fef35eba02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510911} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd2d0dc-499c-5f6b-a3b3-eabdda3e2c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510930} {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2bb1-9c60-5fec-9eec-8ac3b0f23491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511139} {:answer/id #uuid "abff3922-0963-57a5-a0b4-d3c74b7e0c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abff3922-0963-57a5-a0b4-d3c74b7e0c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511141} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c19081a-fb06-5be4-b08f-6665e6611016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89569c74-5681-5d64-867b-d3414ad5af76" :question/math-academy-id "q-48431" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=1?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ k, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-3}{kx-k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-1)}{k(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x-1)}}{k\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x+3}{k}=\\frac{4}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ we need $k=\\pm 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2d497f-046e-5ff8-9cc4-36ed95644117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099802} {:answer/id #uuid "0c60f8f4-7300-5616-8f6e-c3d7dff732be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510803} {:answer/id #uuid "a78a608b-de9f-5aa1-a04e-3fd289b0efef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511137} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e5d373-36bb-58f3-a752-14e6513fd5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511193} {:answer/id #uuid "e27fce12-ba0e-569d-9f95-ba338484dfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27fce12-ba0e-569d-9f95-ba338484dfd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511253} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d7abef-1e5f-5e7e-a713-da16f17f6fd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab43e107-00fc-5630-8abd-2dc86272117f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48743" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k}, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ k, & x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k} & =\\frac{(x+3)(x+2)}{k(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{k\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=-2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}(\\frac{x+3}{k})=\\frac{1}{k}.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -2}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ k & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we need $k=\\pm 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cd56ebd-30ee-5acf-b691-5c2e5f485b52" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Point Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c74c4e-8b61-5db7-8ead-406f11f4ccb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510798} {:answer/id #uuid "6379ff3d-9103-53e6-bcef-4d40c4bdcece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510993} {:answer/id #uuid "77ea4d29-9341-5395-8064-d4d2e262dd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511029} {:answer/id #uuid "81870a9d-5407-5ab4-b674-61633547aec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511047} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71f82c-bec2-5150-8281-5e8f0c047fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77ea4d29-9341-5395-8064-d4d2e262dd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511029} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9472cfa-409a-5fa2-b496-e7f99f19db09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d55624d-78c7-5fb3-b79b-1987319cc6a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-48747" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8}, & x\\ne 2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-2)}{(x-4)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{(x-4)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then can compute the limit at $x=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2}{x-4}=\\frac{2+2}{2-4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFor $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-12 & =-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3k-10 & =0 \\\\ (k+2)(k-5) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the function to be continuous at $x=2,$ we need $k=-2$ or $k=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510822} {:answer/id #uuid "17d0dd42-5a1b-52aa-becb-f58c46712b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510835} {:answer/id #uuid "560d4297-737e-51ee-b485-c82f9de8306b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510965} {:answer/id #uuid "84972923-41cf-5a0d-9170-7092398d68bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511055} {:answer/id #uuid "93fd1a29-955a-551f-9dee-8f81ad48e8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $k$ exists" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a86203-0bc5-55e2-986d-c1c309e6ee38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510822} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee0499b8-2a39-5262-89e9-bfc3b9bddd29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a4ad86f-33ac-54b8-ac3c-2f4008b70bee" :question/math-academy-id "q-48751" :question/problem "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-3?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k}, & x<-3 \\\\ -\\frac{kx}{3}, & x\\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $g(x)$, the left-sided limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{{x}^{2}+14x+33}{2kx+6k} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)(x+3)}{2k(x+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{(x+11)\\cancel{(x+3)}}{2k\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}\\frac{x+11}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-\\frac{kx}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(-3)}{3} \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $g(-3)=k.$ \n\nSo, for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-3,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}g(x) \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{k} \\\\ {k}^{2} & =4 \\\\ k & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-2$ or $k=2$ remove the discontinuity at $x=-3.$"}]}
